\[\langle D_{u}G(\lambda,\mathbf{u})\cdot\mathbf{v},\boldsymbol{\phi}\rangle=\lambda \sum_{j=1}^{N}\,\{\langle u_{j}\partial\mathbf{v}/\partial x_{j},\boldsymbol{ \phi}\rangle+\langle v_{j}\partial\mathbf{u}/\partial x_{j},\boldsymbol{\phi} \rangle\}.\]

But

\[\langle u_{j}\partial\mathbf{v}/\partial x_{j},\boldsymbol{\phi}\rangle=- \langle\mathbf{v},\partial(u_{j}\boldsymbol{\phi})/\partial x_{j}\rangle;\]

hence

\[|\langle u_{j}\partial\mathbf{v}/\partial x_{j},\boldsymbol{\phi}\rangle| \leqslant C_{1}\,\|\,\mathbf{v}\|_{0,\,\mathcal{Q}}\,\|\,u_{j}\|_{2,\, \mathcal{Q}}\,\|\,\Phi\|_{1,\,\mathcal{Q}},\]

with a similar bound for \(\langle v_{j}\partial\mathbf{u}/\partial x_{j},\boldsymbol{\phi}\rangle\). As a consequence, \(D_{u}G(\lambda,\mathbf{u})\) can be extended to a continuous linear operator from \(L^{2}(\Omega)^{N}\) into \(H^{-1}(\Omega)^{N}\), provided \(\mathbf{u}\) belongs to \(\mathrm{H}^{2}(\Omega)^{N}\). This settles (3.47).

As far as (3.48) is concerned, we make use of Remark 3.7 and the compactness of \(X\) into \(H\).

It remains to verify (3.50): \(D_{u}F(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\) is an isomorphism of \(H\). By assumption, we already know that:

\[D_{u}F(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))=I+TD_{u}G(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\]

is an isomorphism of \(X\). In addition, we have just seen that \(TD_{u}G(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\in\mathcal{L}(L^{2}(\Omega)^{N};V)\) whenever \(\mathbf{u}(\lambda)\in H^{2}(\Omega)^{N}\). Therefore, the compactness of the imbedding of \(H^{1}(\Omega)\) into \(L^{2}(\Omega)\) implies that \(TD_{u}G(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\) is a compact operator from \(L^{2}(\Omega)^{N}\) into itself. Hence we can apply to \(D_{u}F(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\) Fredholm's alternative, namely \(D_{u}F(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\) is an isomorphism of \(H\) iff the equation

(4.13) \[D_{u}F(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\cdot\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}\quad\text{with }\mathbf{v}\quad\text{in }H\]

has only the zero solution. But if \(\mathbf{v}\) in \(H\) satisfies (4.13), then \(\mathbf{v}\) belongs to \(V\) since \(\mathbf{v}=-TD_{u}G(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\cdot\mathbf{v}\). Therefore \(\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}\) because \(D_{u}F(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\) is an isomorphism of \(X\). This proves (3.50).

Consequently, we can apply the conclusion of Theorem 3.5: if the mapping \(\lambda\to\mathbf{u}(\lambda)\) is continuous from \(\Lambda\) into \([H^{1}_{0}(\Omega)\cap H^{2}(\Omega)]^{N}\), then for all \(h\) sufficiently small, we have:

\[\|\mathbf{u}_{h}(\lambda)-\mathbf{u}(\lambda)\|_{0,\,\Omega}\leqslant C_{2}\, \{\|\,(T-T_{h})G(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\|_{0,\,\Omega}+|\mathbf{u}_{h}( \lambda)-\mathbf{u}(\lambda)|_{1,\,\Omega}^{2}\}.\]

Thus, when \((\mathbf{u}(\lambda),p(\lambda))\) belongs to \(H^{m+1}(\Omega)^{N}\times H^{m}(\Omega)\), the bound (II.1.50) gives:

\[\|(T-T_{h})G(\lambda,\mathbf{u}(\lambda))\|_{0,\,\Omega}\leqslant C_{3}\, \mathbf{h}^{m+1}\{\,\|\,\mathbf{u}(\lambda)\|_{m+1,\,\Omega}+\|\,p(\lambda)\|_ {m,\,\Omega}\}\]

and (4.12) stems from this estimate and (4.7). 

Roughly speaking, Theorems 4.1 and 4.2 can be summarized by saying that all the function spaces introduced in Paragraphs II.2 and II.3 to solve the Stokes equations can be also applied to approximate branches of nonsingular solutions of the Navier-Stokes problem with a _similar accuracy_. For instance, assume that the two-dimensional Navier-Stokes equations (4.1) have a branch of nonsingular solutions with the regularity:

